[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: INVARIANT is a requirement and does not survive as its own type

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-INVARIANT-IS-A-REQUIREMENT
TITLE: INVARIANT is a requirement and does not survive as its own type
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATUS: accepted
RETIRES_ON: An invariant is found that is not normative, or not universal.
STATEMENT: >>>
INVARIANT is a REQUIREMENT. The type does not survive the migration to
DEC-NODE-TYPE-GRID's vocabulary; its four nodes become requirements.

It falls out of the axes with nothing left over. An invariant is normative --
it can be violated, and when reality disagrees the world is what gets fixed --
and it is universal, holding across every case rather than describing one. That
is the requirement cell exactly. The grid already said as much by omission, in
ruling that a constraint is a requirement rather than a type of its own; this
states it rather than leaving it to be read.

Nothing is lost. The distinguishing flavour of an invariant -- that it is the
rule rather than the interface, so it can be violated while every interface is
satisfied -- is a property of its statement, not of its type.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The third and last of the types DEC-NODE-TYPE-GRID left unsaid, and the cheapest: four nodes, and the axes give one answer with no residue. Settling it now means the migration inherits three rulings rather than three arguments.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Ruled by the operator on 2026-08-28. AUTHORED_BY reads llm because the writer has no flag for it -- MECH-RUNTIME-WRITE-GUARD makes that key the operator's, which is itself recorded as misplaced in MECH-MILESTONE-TWO-SEMANTICS-MISPLACED. The other two types are read, not ruled: MECH-SLICE-IS-WORK-NOT-A-USE-CASE and MECH-SPIKE-IS-A-PIPELINE-TO-A-DECISION.
<<<
